                    "type": "output_text",
                    "text": "Here’s a practical way to ask for an extension, plus ready-to-use templates you can adapt.\n\nKey tips\n- Ask early. Don’t wait until the last minute.\n- Check the course policy first (late policies, allowable extensions, any forms).\n- Be specific. Propose a new deadline you can meet.\n- Show progress. Mention what you’ve completed and what remains.\n- Keep it concise and respectful. Acknowledge you understand extensions aren’t always possible.\n- Offer alternatives if an extension isn’t allowed (partial submission, different assignment, etc.).\n\nWhat to include in your message\n- A clear subject line (if email)\n- A brief greeting\n- A short reason for the request (without oversharing)\n- A proposed new deadline\n- What you’ve already completed and your plan to finish\n- Acknowledgement of policy and appreciation\n\nTemplates\n\n1) Formal email to professor\nSubject: Request for extension on Problem Set [number] for [Course name]\n\nDear Professor [Last Name],\n\nI hope you’re well. I am writing to request a short extension for Problem Set [number], which is currently due on [original due date].\n\nThe reason for this request is [brief reason, e.g., a heavy workload this week / illness / a personal matter]. I want to ensure I can submit work that reflects my best effort. If possible, could the deadline be extended to [proposed new due date]?\n\nSo far, I have completed [briefly describe progress, e.g., problems 1–3 and part of 4] and plan to complete [what remains] by the extended date. If an extension isn’t possible, I will submit by the original deadline, but I would be grateful for any alternative you might suggest (e.g., partial credit for the portion completed).\n\nThank you for considering this request.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]\n[Course and section]\n[Student ID] (optional)\n\n2) Short/concise message to TA or via course platform\nHi [TA’s name], I’m requesting a short extension for Problem Set [number], due [date]. Due to [reason], I’d be able to submit by [new date]. I’ve completed [brief progress]. If an extension isn’t possible, I’ll submit by the original deadline. Thanks for considering.\n\n3) In-person or quick chat\n“Hey Professor [Last Name], do you have a moment? I wanted to ask about possibly getting a brief extension on Problem Set [number]. I’m behind a bit due to [reason], and I think I could produce a stronger submission if I could move the due date to [new date]. I’ve already completed [brief progress]. I understand if extensions aren’t possible, but I wanted to check.”\n\nIf you have documentation\n- If you have a medical note or official documentation, mention you can provide it if needed.\n- Keep a simple line: “I can share documentation if that would help.”\n\nWhat to avoid\n- Don’t push for extensions repeatedly or after being denied.\n- Don’t fabricate reasons; be honest.\n- Don’t imply you’ll do worse unless you’re granted more time.\n- Don’t demand a specific outcome; be polite and flexible.\n\nIf an extension is denied\n- Ask about the policy for partial credit or alternative assignments.\n- Consider requesting a smaller extension or a revised deadline for a subset of the problems.\n- Plan to submit by the original deadline with the best quality you can achieve.\n\nIf you want, tell me your course, assignment details, and your reason. I can tailor a precise email for you.",
